Let $\mathcal{L}$ denote the class of all bounded linear operators between Banach spaces. A Banach operator ideal $(\mathcal{A},\alpha)$ is called classical if its operator-ideal norm $\alpha$ coincides with the usual operator norm $\|\cdot\|$ on $\mathcal{A}$. For a Banach operator ideal $\mathcal{A}$, a Banach space $X$ is said to have the metric approximation property with respect to $\mathcal{A}$ (MAP for $\mathcal{A}$) if the identity operator $\mathrm{id}_X$ can be approximated, uniformly on compact subsets of $X$, by a net of finite-rank operators $(S_\lambda)$ with $\sup_\lambda \alpha(S_\lambda)\le 1$ (equivalently here, $\sup_\lambda\|S_\lambda\|\le 1$).

Consider the following classical Banach operator ideals:
- $\mathrm{RN}$: the ideal of Radon–Nikodým operators (operators $T:X\to Y$ such that $T$ factors through, or whose image measures satisfy, the Radon–Nikodým property);
- $\mathrm{V}$: the ideal of completely continuous (Dunford–Pettis) operators, i.e., operators mapping weakly null sequences to norm null sequences;
- $\mathrm{U}$: the ideal of unconditionally summing operators, i.e., operators $T$ such that $\sum T x_n$ converges unconditionally whenever $\sum x_n$ is weakly unconditionally Cauchy;
- $\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{dual}}$ and $\mathrm{U}^{\mathrm{dual}}$: the dual ideals of $\mathrm{V}$ and $\mathrm{U}$ respectively, where for a Banach operator ideal $\mathcal{A}$ the dual ideal $\mathcal{A}^{\mathrm{dual}}$ consists of all operators $T$ whose adjoint $T^*$ belongs to $\mathcal{A}$.

Give an intrinsic characterization (description) of those Banach spaces $X$ that have the metric approximation property with respect to each of the ideals $\mathrm{RN}$, $\mathrm{V}$, $\mathrm{U}$, $\mathrm{V}^{\mathrm{dual}}$, and $\mathrm{U}^{\mathrm{dual}}$.

